[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Out-of-range "enum" values across the FFI boundary
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The C API declares **no enums**. The equivalent unchecked-integer inputs that a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m caller can push across the boundary are covered above:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `int count` outside `[0, 10]` → rows 12, 13, 14 (and the excluded case below).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `int idx1`/`idx2` outside `[0, count)` → rows 8, 9, 10, 11.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `operation_func op` — an arbitrary pointer-sized value; `NULL` is row 22.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Rows 1–2 and `cfg_foreach_*` cover every *valid* variant, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `cfg_foreach_cross_abi` passes the C `.so`'s function pointers into the Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `process_with_foreach` (and vice versa) to prove the callback ABI matches.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `double d` outside the `int` range, plus `±Inf` / NaN → rows 3, 4, 5.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Conditions deliberately NOT differential-tested (unbounded memory corruption)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These are reachable but produce non-deterministic out-of-bounds *writes*, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m "identical output" is not a meaningful assertion for either side. Both
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m implementations perform the same unchecked arithmetic; documented for
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m completeness.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | condition | why excluded |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |-----------|--------------|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `process_with_foreach` with `arr->count < 0` | `FOREACH` compares `count_iter != size`; a negative `size` is never reached going upward, so it writes `item->scaled` / `item->value` for ~2^31 increasing out-of-bounds indices before wrapping. Both sides scribble over the heap and die at an implementation-defined address. |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `compute_weighted_sum` / `process_with_foreach` with `arr->count > 10` | Reads/writes past `data[10]`. Only reachable if the caller writes `count` directly (`init_result_array` clamps it). Tested only up to the in-bounds maximum of 10. |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `compare_results_in_array` with a wildly negative index (e.g. `INT32_MIN`) | `arr->data + INT32_MIN` overflows the pointer; no dereference occurs, but the address is not representable. Tested with small negative indices (row 11) where both sides agree. |
